#!/usr/bin/env perl
use 5.014;
use strict;
use warnings;

use Term::Chess;

our $VERSION = '0.03';

exit Term::Chess->run(@ARGV);

__END__

=head1 NAME

chess - chess on a terminal, against a bot

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    chess                        # play the bot from the opening
    chess --level 0 --ascii      # the weakest rung, the FEN letters
    chess --seat p2              # play Black
    chess --fen 'FEN' --no-pick  # a position, typed moves

=head1 DESCRIPTION

A front end and nothing else: every decision it makes is an option, and the
board, the picker and the typed prompt live in L<Term::Chess>. The rules are
L<Chess::Plisco>'s and the opponent is Plisco's own search, bounded in nodes.

C<--level> takes either a rung index into C<@Term::Chess::Bot::LADDER> or a
node budget outright, and tells them apart by size. Left out, the game's seed
draws a rung.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

C<0> if you won, the game drew, or you quit; C<1> if you lost; C<2> for a bad
option or a refused value.

=cut
